module.exports = {
  title: 'Directives & Template Features',
  slug: 'directives',
  order: 4,
  topics: [
    {
      title: 'Conditional Rendering and Lists: v-if, v-show, v-for',
      slug: 'conditional-rendering-lists',
      order: 0,
      content: [
        `<h2>Why it matters</h2>`,
        `<p>Three questions from this topic appear in virtually every Vue interview: <code>v-if</code> vs <code>v-show</code>, why <code>:key</code> is required in <code>v-for</code>, and why you must not put <code>v-if</code> and <code>v-for</code> on the same element. All three have precise, mechanical answers, and all three have real performance or correctness consequences when you get them wrong.</p>`,
        `<p>The <code>:key</code> question in particular is the highest-value item in this chapter. A missing or wrongly chosen key produces bugs that look like sorcery — checkboxes that tick the wrong row after a sort, inputs that keep the previous item's text, animations that play on the wrong element. Every one of those is the diffing algorithm doing exactly what it was told.</p>`,
        `<h2>The concept, simply</h2>`,
        `<p><strong><code>v-if</code></strong> controls whether an element is <em>created</em>. When false, the element and its component subtree do not exist in the DOM at all.</p>`,
        `<p><strong><code>v-show</code></strong> always creates the element and toggles <code>display: none</code>. The DOM node is always there.</p>`,
        `<p><strong><code>v-for</code></strong> renders a list, and needs a <code>:key</code> so Vue can match old elements to new ones between renders.</p>`,
        `<table>`,
        `<thead><tr><th></th><th><code>v-if</code></th><th><code>v-show</code></th></tr></thead>`,
        `<tbody>`,
        `<tr><td>When false</td><td>Not in the DOM</td><td>In the DOM, <code>display: none</code></td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td>Initial cost</td><td>Zero if false</td><td>Always rendered</td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td>Toggle cost</td><td>High — mount/unmount, lifecycle hooks run</td><td>Trivial — one CSS property</td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td>Component state on toggle</td><td>Destroyed and rebuilt</td><td>Preserved</td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td>Works on <code>&lt;template&gt;</code></td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td>Use for</td><td>Rarely toggled; expensive; may never render</td><td>Frequently toggled; cheap; needs to keep state</td></tr>`,
        `</tbody>`,
        `</table>`,
        `<p>The rule of thumb: <strong>toggles often → <code>v-show</code>; might never render → <code>v-if</code>.</strong> A tab panel switched constantly is <code>v-show</code>; an admin-only dashboard section most users never see is <code>v-if</code>.</p>`,
        `<h2>How it works</h2>`,
        `<p><strong><code>v-if</code> compiles to a ternary</strong> in the render function, producing either the element's vnode or a placeholder comment node. That placeholder is why toggling causes a real mount: the subtree is constructed from scratch, <code>setup</code> runs, <code>onMounted</code> fires, and any component state inside is new. <code>v-show</code> compiles to a <code>style.display</code> binding — no structural change at all, so lifecycle hooks do not re-run and state survives.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Why <code>:key</code> matters</strong> is the heart of the topic. When a list re-renders, Vue diffs the old array of vnodes against the new one. Without keys it uses an <em>in-place patch</em> strategy: it walks both lists by index and patches position 0 against position 0, position 1 against position 1, and so on. That is fast and correct <em>only</em> if elements never change position. Insert an item at the front, sort the list, or filter it, and every position now holds different data — so Vue patches the text of existing DOM nodes rather than moving them.</p>`,
        `<p>The rendered text ends up right, because Vue overwrites it. What does <em>not</em> get fixed is any state the DOM node owns and Vue does not manage: the value typed into an <code>&lt;input&gt;</code>, which checkbox is ticked, which element has focus, scroll position, and the internal state of any child component at that position. This is why the bug always shows up as "the checkboxes are on the wrong rows after sorting."</p>`,
        `{{fig:ch5-vfor-key-diffing.svg|Two list diffs after prepending an item: without keys Vue patches by index so every row's DOM node is reused with new text and checkbox state stays on the wrong row; with keys Vue matches by identity and moves the existing nodes|Without keys Vue patches by position; with keys it matches by identity and moves nodes, preserving each row's DOM state.}}`,
        `<p>With a stable <code>:key</code>, Vue matches vnodes by identity instead of position, so it <em>moves</em> the existing DOM node and its state travels with it. The key must be <strong>unique among siblings and stable across renders</strong> — a database id, a UUID, a slug. Using the array index defeats the whole mechanism, because the index <em>is</em> the position.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Why not <code>v-if</code> and <code>v-for</code> together?</strong> In Vue 3 <code>v-if</code> has the <em>higher</em> priority, so the condition is evaluated before the loop variable exists — <code>v-if="item.active"</code> throws because <code>item</code> is undefined. (In Vue 2 the priority was reversed, which "worked" but re-evaluated the condition for every item on every render.) Either way it is a bug or a waste; the fix is a computed that filters, or a <code>&lt;template v-for&gt;</code> wrapping a <code>v-if</code>.</p>`,
        `<p><strong><code>v-for</code> details</strong> worth knowing: it iterates arrays, objects (giving <code>value, key, index</code>), integers (1..n), strings and any iterable; <code>of</code> and <code>in</code> are interchangeable; and it can go on <code>&lt;template&gt;</code> to repeat a group without a wrapper element — in which case the <code>:key</code> goes on the <code>&lt;template&gt;</code>.</p>`,
        `<h2>Code examples</h2>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;template&gt;\n  &lt;!-- v-if / v-else-if / v-else must be adjacent siblings --&gt;\n  &lt;LoadingSpinner v-if="loading" /&gt;\n  &lt;ErrorPanel v-else-if="error" :error="error" /&gt;\n  &lt;EmptyState v-else-if="!items.length" /&gt;\n  &lt;ItemList v-else :items="items" /&gt;\n\n  &lt;!-- v-if on &lt;template&gt; groups elements without adding a wrapper --&gt;\n  &lt;template v-if="isAdmin"&gt;\n    &lt;h2&gt;Admin tools&lt;/h2&gt;\n    &lt;AuditLog /&gt;\n  &lt;/template&gt;\n\n  &lt;!-- v-show: stays in the DOM. Right for something toggled constantly. --&gt;\n  &lt;aside v-show="sidebarOpen" class="sidebar"&gt;…&lt;/aside&gt;\n&lt;/template&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;template&gt;\n  &lt;!-- ✅ Stable, unique key --&gt;\n  &lt;li v-for="user in users" :key="user.id"&gt;\n    {{ user.name }}\n    &lt;input v-model="user.note"&gt;   &lt;!-- keeps its value across sorts --&gt;\n  &lt;/li&gt;\n\n  &lt;!-- index is available as the second argument --&gt;\n  &lt;li v-for="(user, index) in users" :key="user.id"&gt;\n    {{ index + 1 }}. {{ user.name }}\n  &lt;/li&gt;\n\n  &lt;!-- Objects: value, key, index --&gt;\n  &lt;li v-for="(value, key, index) in settings" :key="key"&gt;\n    {{ index }} — {{ key }}: {{ value }}\n  &lt;/li&gt;\n\n  &lt;!-- A range (1..5) --&gt;\n  &lt;span v-for="n in 5" :key="n"&gt;{{ n }}&lt;/span&gt;\n\n  &lt;!-- Repeat a GROUP without a wrapper element — key goes on the template --&gt;\n  &lt;template v-for="item in items" :key="item.id"&gt;\n    &lt;dt&gt;{{ item.term }}&lt;/dt&gt;\n    &lt;dd&gt;{{ item.definition }}&lt;/dd&gt;\n  &lt;/template&gt;\n&lt;/template&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<p>The <code>v-if</code> + <code>v-for</code> problem, and the two correct fixes:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;!-- ❌ Throws in Vue 3: v-if runs first, so \`user\` doesn't exist yet --&gt;\n&lt;li v-for="user in users" v-if="user.active" :key="user.id"&gt;{{ user.name }}&lt;/li&gt;\n\n&lt;!-- ✅ Fix 1 (preferred): filter in a computed — the loop only sees what it renders --&gt;\n&lt;script setup&gt;\nconst activeUsers = computed(() =&gt; users.value.filter(u =&gt; u.active))\n&lt;/script&gt;\n&lt;template&gt;\n  &lt;li v-for="user in activeUsers" :key="user.id"&gt;{{ user.name }}&lt;/li&gt;\n&lt;/template&gt;\n\n&lt;!-- ✅ Fix 2: separate the concerns onto different elements --&gt;\n&lt;template v-for="user in users" :key="user.id"&gt;\n  &lt;li v-if="user.active"&gt;{{ user.name }}&lt;/li&gt;\n&lt;/template&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<p>The index-as-key bug, made concrete:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;script setup&gt;\nconst todos = ref([\n  { id: 1, text: 'Buy milk', done: false },\n  { id: 2, text: 'Walk dog', done: false },\n])\n\nfunction addToFront() {\n  todos.value.unshift({ id: 3, text: 'New', done: false })\n}\n&lt;/script&gt;\n\n&lt;template&gt;\n  &lt;!-- ❌ Tick \"Walk dog\", then addToFront().\n       The tick stays at position 1 — now on \"Buy milk\". --&gt;\n  &lt;li v-for="(todo, i) in todos" :key="i"&gt;\n    &lt;input type="checkbox"&gt; {{ todo.text }}\n  &lt;/li&gt;\n\n  &lt;!-- ✅ The tick follows the row it belongs to --&gt;\n  &lt;li v-for="todo in todos" :key="todo.id"&gt;\n    &lt;input type="checkbox"&gt; {{ todo.text }}\n  &lt;/li&gt;\n&lt;/template&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<p><code>:key</code> also works outside <code>v-for</code> — to force a remount:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;!-- Changing the key destroys and recreates the component, so it refetches\n     and resets its internal state when the route param changes. --&gt;\n&lt;UserProfile :key="route.params.id" :user-id="route.params.id" /&gt;\n\n&lt;!-- Same trick to stop v-if/v-else reusing an input between branches --&gt;\n&lt;input v-if="loginMode" key="username" placeholder="Username"&gt;\n&lt;input v-else key="email" placeholder="Email"&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<h2>Common mistakes &amp; gotchas</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><strong>Using the array index as <code>:key</code>.</strong> Equivalent to no key at all whenever the list can be reordered, filtered or inserted into. Only acceptable for a static list that never changes.</li>`,
        `<li><strong><code>v-if</code> and <code>v-for</code> on the same element.</strong> Throws in Vue 3 because <code>v-if</code> is evaluated first. Filter in a computed instead.</li>`,
        `<li><strong><code>v-show</code> on a <code>&lt;template&gt;</code>.</strong> There is no element to set <code>display</code> on, so it does nothing.</li>`,
        `<li><strong><code>v-show</code> for something expensive.</strong> It always renders — including the initial cost you were trying to avoid.</li>`,
        `<li><strong><code>v-if</code> for a frequently toggled panel.</strong> Every toggle destroys and rebuilds a subtree, re-runs lifecycle hooks and loses form state.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Non-adjacent <code>v-else</code>.</strong> <code>v-else</code>/<code>v-else-if</code> must immediately follow their <code>v-if</code>; anything in between breaks the chain.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Mutating the array you are iterating.</strong> Splicing inside the loop's own handler produces skipped items; operate on a copy or filter into a computed.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Assuming <code>v-if="false"</code> means the component's <code>onUnmounted</code> ran cleanly.</strong> It does — but any interval it started still needs releasing there, and that is easy to forget when a subtree is toggled often.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Duplicate keys.</strong> Vue warns, and the diff behaves unpredictably. Keys must be unique among siblings.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>Best practices</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li>Always give <code>v-for</code> a stable, unique <code>:key</code> from the data — an id, not an index.</li>`,
        `<li>Choose between <code>v-if</code> and <code>v-show</code> by toggle frequency and by whether the content may never be needed.</li>`,
        `<li>Filter and sort in computeds, not in the template — cached, testable and it keeps <code>v-if</code> out of the loop.</li>`,
        `<li>Use <code>&lt;template v-if&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;template v-for&gt;</code> to group elements without polluting the DOM with wrapper divs.</li>`,
        `<li>Use <code>:key</code> deliberately outside <code>v-for</code> when you actually want a remount — a route-driven detail component is the classic case.</li>`,
        `<li>Keep <code>v-if</code>/<code>v-else-if</code> chains short; past three branches, <code>&lt;component :is&gt;</code> with a lookup object is clearer.</li>`,
        `<li>Enable <code>eslint-plugin-vue</code>'s <code>require-v-for-key</code> and <code>no-use-v-if-with-v-for</code> rules and let the linter enforce all of this.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>⭐ Interview lens</h2>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "<code>v-if</code> vs <code>v-show</code>?"</strong><br>A: <code>v-if</code> is conditional <em>rendering</em> — when false the element is not in the DOM, so there is no initial cost, but every toggle mounts and unmounts a subtree, runs lifecycle hooks and loses internal state. <code>v-show</code> always renders and toggles <code>display: none</code>, so toggling is a single CSS change and state is preserved, but you pay the render cost even if it is never shown. Frequent toggling → <code>v-show</code>; might never render, or expensive → <code>v-if</code>. Also, <code>v-show</code> doesn't work on <code>&lt;template&gt;</code>.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Why does <code>v-for</code> need a <code>:key</code>?"</strong><br>A: To let the diff match old vnodes to new ones by identity rather than position. Without keys Vue patches in place by index — position 0 against position 0 — which is correct only if nothing moves. Prepend or sort, and each position now holds different data, so Vue reuses the existing DOM nodes and just rewrites their text. The rendered text ends up right, but anything the DOM node owns and Vue doesn't manage travels with the node instead of the data: input values, checkbox state, focus, scroll position, child component state. With a stable key, Vue moves the node and its state goes with it.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Can you use the index as a key?" (trap)</strong><br>A: Only for a list that never reorders, filters or has items inserted or removed anywhere but the end — and at that point you may as well use a real id. The index <em>is</em> the position, so keying by it gives Vue the same information it had with no key at all and reproduces exactly the bug keys exist to prevent.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Why can't <code>v-if</code> and <code>v-for</code> be on the same element?"</strong><br>A: In Vue 3, <code>v-if</code> has higher priority and is evaluated first, so the loop variable doesn't exist yet — <code>v-if="item.active"</code> throws on an undefined <code>item</code>. In Vue 2 the precedence was reversed, so it worked but re-evaluated the condition for every item on every render, which is wasteful. Either way, the right fix is to filter with a computed, or put the <code>v-for</code> on a <code>&lt;template&gt;</code> and the <code>v-if</code> on the inner element.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "How would you force a component to reset when a route param changes?"</strong><br>A: Give it a <code>:key</code> bound to the param — <code>&lt;UserProfile :key="route.params.id" /&gt;</code>. A changed key means a different vnode identity, so Vue unmounts the old instance and mounts a fresh one, which re-runs setup and refetches. It's the same mechanism as <code>v-for</code> keys, used deliberately for a remount rather than to preserve identity.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<h2>Cheat-card</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><code>v-if</code> = not in the DOM; toggle = mount/unmount + lifecycle + state loss. <code>v-show</code> = <code>display:none</code>; state preserved.</li>`,
        `<li>Toggles often → <code>v-show</code>. May never render / expensive → <code>v-if</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><code>v-show</code> doesn't work on <code>&lt;template&gt;</code>; <code>v-if</code> does.</li>`,
        `<li><code>:key</code> must be <strong>unique among siblings and stable</strong> — an id, never the index.</li>`,
        `<li>No key ⇒ in-place patch by index ⇒ input/checkbox/focus/child state stuck to the wrong row.</li>`,
        `<li>Never <code>v-if</code> + <code>v-for</code> on one element — <code>v-if</code> wins in Vue 3 and the item is undefined. Filter in a computed.</li>`,
        `<li><code>v-for</code> iterates arrays, objects (<code>value, key, index</code>), integers, strings, iterables.</li>`,
        `<li><code>&lt;template v-for&gt;</code> repeats a group; the <code>:key</code> goes on the <code>&lt;template&gt;</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>Changing <code>:key</code> on a component forces a remount — useful for route-driven resets.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
      ].join('\n'),
    },

    {
      title: 'Forms and v-model',
      slug: 'v-model-forms',
      order: 1,
      content: [
        `<h2>Why it matters</h2>`,
        `<p><code>v-model</code> is the feature people cite when they say Vue is pleasant to work with, and it is the one every interviewer asks you to <em>de-sugar</em>. "What does <code>v-model</code> compile to?" is a real question with a real answer, and it changes depending on the element — which is exactly the detail candidates miss.</p>`,
        `<p>Forms are also where most business applications spend their complexity. Knowing the modifiers, knowing why <code>.lazy</code> exists, and knowing how to build a component that supports <code>v-model</code> correctly is directly useful from day one.</p>`,
        `<h2>The concept, simply</h2>`,
        `<p><code>v-model</code> creates a <strong>two-way binding</strong>: the input displays the state, and typing updates the state. It is sugar for a value binding plus an event listener.</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;input v-model="message"&gt;\n\n&lt;!-- is exactly --&gt;\n&lt;input :value="message" @input="message = $event.target.value"&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<p>The critical detail: <strong>which property and which event depends on the element type.</strong></p>`,
        `<table>`,
        `<thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Property bound</th><th>Event listened for</th></tr></thead>`,
        `<tbody>`,
        `<tr><td><code>&lt;input type="text"&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;textarea&gt;</code></td><td><code>value</code></td><td><code>input</code></td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td><code>&lt;input type="checkbox"&gt;</code>, <code>radio</code></td><td><code>checked</code></td><td><code>change</code></td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td><code>&lt;select&gt;</code></td><td><code>value</code></td><td><code>change</code></td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td>A component</td><td><code>modelValue</code> prop</td><td><code>update:modelValue</code></td></tr>`,
        `</tbody>`,
        `</table>`,
        `{{fig:ch5-vmodel.svg|Diagram of the v-model two-way loop: state binds to the element's value property, a user event updates the state, and the state change re-renders the element, with the component variant using modelValue and update:modelValue|v-model is a value binding plus an event listener — which property and which event depends on the element.}}`,
        `<h2>How it works</h2>`,
        `<p>The compiler recognises <code>v-model</code> on a native element and emits the right pair for that element's type. On a component it emits the <code>modelValue</code>/<code>update:modelValue</code> pair instead, which is why any component can support it just by declaring those two things (or, from 3.4, by calling <code>defineModel()</code>).</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Modifiers</strong> adjust the transformation between element and state:</p>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><code>.lazy</code> — sync on <code>change</code> instead of <code>input</code>, so the state updates on blur or Enter rather than on every keystroke. Useful when each update triggers expensive work.</li>`,
        `<li><code>.number</code> — cast to a number with <code>parseFloat</code>. Essential because <code>&lt;input type="number"&gt;</code> still gives you a <em>string</em>. If the parse fails the original string is kept.</li>`,
        `<li><code>.trim</code> — strip leading and trailing whitespace.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<p>Custom components can define their own modifiers: <code>defineModel()</code> returns modifier information, and the manual pattern receives them as a <code>modelModifiers</code> prop.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Checkboxes</strong> have two distinct behaviours. Bound to a boolean, a single checkbox sets <code>true</code>/<code>false</code> — customisable with <code>true-value</code>/<code>false-value</code>. Bound to an <em>array</em>, multiple checkboxes push and remove their <code>value</code> from that array. Radios bind to a single value; the shared state variable is what makes them mutually exclusive, with no <code>name</code> attribute required.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Selects with object values</strong> need <code>:value</code> rather than <code>value</code>, because an HTML attribute can only hold a string. With <code>:value="user"</code> the bound state becomes the actual object.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Why <code>v-model</code> does not work on a prop.</strong> <code>v-model="someProp"</code> would compile to an assignment to the prop, which is forbidden. The fix is a writable computed whose setter emits, or <code>defineModel()</code>, which does that for you.</p>`,
        `<h2>Code examples</h2>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;script setup&gt;\nimport { reactive } from 'vue'\n\nconst form = reactive({\n  name: '',\n  bio: '',\n  age: null,\n  newsletter: false,\n  interests: [],       // array → multiple checkboxes\n  plan: 'free',        // radio group\n  country: null,       // select with an object value\n  tags: [],            // multi-select\n})\n\nconst countries = [{ code: 'IN', label: 'India' }, { code: 'US', label: 'USA' }]\n&lt;/script&gt;\n\n&lt;template&gt;\n  &lt;form @submit.prevent="submit"&gt;\n    &lt;!-- text: trim whitespace as the user types --&gt;\n    &lt;input v-model.trim="form.name" placeholder="Name"&gt;\n\n    &lt;!-- lazy: sync on change/blur, not on every keystroke --&gt;\n    &lt;textarea v-model.lazy="form.bio"&gt;&lt;/textarea&gt;\n\n    &lt;!-- number: without this you get the STRING \"42\" --&gt;\n    &lt;input v-model.number="form.age" type="number"&gt;\n\n    &lt;!-- single checkbox → boolean --&gt;\n    &lt;label&gt;&lt;input type="checkbox" v-model="form.newsletter"&gt; Subscribe&lt;/label&gt;\n\n    &lt;!-- custom truthy/falsy values --&gt;\n    &lt;input type="checkbox" v-model="form.plan" true-value="pro" false-value="free"&gt;\n\n    &lt;!-- multiple checkboxes → array of values --&gt;\n    &lt;label v-for="t in ['vue', 'react', 'node']" :key="t"&gt;\n      &lt;input type="checkbox" :value="t" v-model="form.interests"&gt; {{ t }}\n    &lt;/label&gt;\n\n    &lt;!-- radios: shared state, no name attribute needed --&gt;\n    &lt;label&gt;&lt;input type="radio" value="free" v-model="form.plan"&gt; Free&lt;/label&gt;\n    &lt;label&gt;&lt;input type="radio" value="pro" v-model="form.plan"&gt; Pro&lt;/label&gt;\n\n    &lt;!-- select with OBJECT values needs :value, not value --&gt;\n    &lt;select v-model="form.country"&gt;\n      &lt;option :value="null" disabled&gt;Choose…&lt;/option&gt;\n      &lt;option v-for="c in countries" :key="c.code" :value="c"&gt;{{ c.label }}&lt;/option&gt;\n    &lt;/select&gt;\n\n    &lt;select v-model="form.tags" multiple&gt;   &lt;!-- binds an array --&gt;\n      &lt;option&gt;design&lt;/option&gt;\n      &lt;option&gt;dev&lt;/option&gt;\n    &lt;/select&gt;\n  &lt;/form&gt;\n&lt;/template&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<p>Making your own component support <code>v-model</code> — three versions:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;!-- ① Vue 3.4+ — the modern way --&gt;\n&lt;script setup&gt;\nconst model = defineModel({ type: String, required: true })\n&lt;/script&gt;\n&lt;template&gt;\n  &lt;input v-model="model" class="input"&gt;\n&lt;/template&gt;\n\n&lt;!-- ② Manual, works on any Vue 3 version --&gt;\n&lt;script setup&gt;\ndefineProps({ modelValue: String })\nconst emit = defineEmits(['update:modelValue'])\n&lt;/script&gt;\n&lt;template&gt;\n  &lt;input :value="modelValue" @input="emit('update:modelValue', $event.target.value)"&gt;\n&lt;/template&gt;\n\n&lt;!-- ③ Writable computed — nicest when the value also needs transforming --&gt;\n&lt;script setup&gt;\nimport { computed } from 'vue'\nconst props = defineProps({ modelValue: String })\nconst emit = defineEmits(['update:modelValue'])\n\nconst value = computed({\n  get: () =&gt; props.modelValue,\n  set: (v) =&gt; emit('update:modelValue', v.toUpperCase()),\n})\n&lt;/script&gt;\n&lt;template&gt;&lt;input v-model="value"&gt;&lt;/template&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<p>Multiple and named models — a date-range picker:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;!-- DateRange.vue --&gt;\n&lt;script setup&gt;\nconst start = defineModel('start')\nconst end = defineModel('end')\n&lt;/script&gt;\n&lt;template&gt;\n  &lt;input type="date" v-model="start"&gt;\n  &lt;input type="date" v-model="end"&gt;\n&lt;/template&gt;\n\n&lt;!-- Parent --&gt;\n&lt;DateRange v-model:start="from" v-model:end="to" /&gt;\n\n&lt;!-- Equivalent to:\n     &lt;DateRange :start="from" @update:start="from = $event"\n                :end="to"     @update:end="to = $event" /&gt; --&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<p>Custom modifiers on a component:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;script setup&gt;\n// Parent writes: &lt;MyInput v-model.capitalize="text" /&gt;\nconst [model, modifiers] = defineModel({\n  set(value) {\n    return modifiers.capitalize ? value.charAt(0).toUpperCase() + value.slice(1) : value\n  },\n})\n&lt;/script&gt;\n&lt;template&gt;&lt;input v-model="model"&gt;&lt;/template&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<h2>Common mistakes &amp; gotchas</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><strong>Forgetting <code>.number</code> on numeric inputs.</strong> <code>&lt;input type="number"&gt;</code> yields a string, so <code>age + 1</code> becomes <code>"251"</code>. This is the most common form bug in Vue.</li>`,
        `<li><strong><code>v-model</code> on a prop.</strong> It compiles to an assignment to the prop and warns. Use <code>defineModel()</code> or a writable computed that emits.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Using <code>value</code> instead of <code>:value</code> for object options.</strong> Attributes are strings, so you get <code>"[object Object]"</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Expecting <code>v-model</code> to also honour a <code>value</code> attribute you wrote.</strong> <code>v-model</code> owns the binding; a literal <code>value</code> on the same element is ignored.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Not initialising array-bound checkboxes to an array.</strong> Starting from <code>null</code> or <code>''</code> gives odd results — start with <code>[]</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Adding <code>name</code> to radios expecting it to do the grouping.</strong> Grouping comes from the shared bound state; <code>name</code> matters only for native form submission.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Forgetting <code>@submit.prevent</code>.</strong> The page reloads and your handler never gets to run.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Assuming IME input works with <code>v-model</code>.</strong> During composition (Chinese, Japanese, Korean input), <code>v-model</code> deliberately does not sync until composition ends; bind <code>input</code> manually if you need every keystroke.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Reaching for a form library too early.</strong> Vue's built-ins plus a computed for validation cover most forms without a dependency.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>Best practices</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li>Always use <code>.number</code> for numeric inputs and <code>.trim</code> for names, emails and free text.</li>`,
        `<li>Use <code>defineModel()</code> for component two-way binding on 3.4+; it is one line and removes a class of mistakes.</li>`,
        `<li>Keep form state in one <code>reactive</code> object — it maps cleanly to a request payload and is easy to reset.</li>`,
        `<li>Derive validation with computeds rather than watchers, so errors recompute automatically and stay cheap.</li>`,
        `<li>Use <code>.lazy</code> when each change triggers expensive work such as a network call — or debounce explicitly.</li>`,
        `<li>Give every input a real <code>&lt;label&gt;</code> and wire <code>aria-invalid</code>/<code>aria-describedby</code> to your error text.</li>`,
        `<li>Consider VeeValidate or FormKit only once schema validation, nested field arrays and async rules are genuinely in play.</li>`,
        `<li>Never trust client validation — it is UX, not a security control.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>⭐ Interview lens</h2>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "What does <code>v-model</code> compile to?"</strong><br>A: A value binding plus an event listener, and the exact pair depends on the element. On a text input it is <code>:value</code> plus <code>@input</code>; on a checkbox or radio, <code>:checked</code> plus <code>@change</code>; on a select, <code>:value</code> plus <code>@change</code>; and on a component, a <code>modelValue</code> prop plus an <code>update:modelValue</code> event. That last form is why any component can support <code>v-model</code> just by declaring those two things.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "How do you add <code>v-model</code> support to a custom component?"</strong><br>A: On 3.4+, <code>const model = defineModel()</code> — it declares the prop and the emit and hands back a writable ref. Before that, declare a <code>modelValue</code> prop and an <code>update:modelValue</code> emit and wire them manually, or wrap them in a writable computed whose getter returns the prop and whose setter emits. Named models — <code>defineModel('start')</code> paired with <code>v-model:start</code> — let one component expose several two-way bindings.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "What do the modifiers do?"</strong><br>A: <code>.lazy</code> switches syncing from <code>input</code> to <code>change</code>, so state updates on blur or Enter rather than every keystroke. <code>.number</code> casts with <code>parseFloat</code>, which you need because <code>&lt;input type="number"&gt;</code> still produces a string. <code>.trim</code> strips surrounding whitespace. Components can define custom modifiers too — <code>defineModel</code> exposes which ones were used.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Why is <code>age + 1</code> giving me <code>'251'</code>?" (trap)</strong><br>A: Because <code>&lt;input type="number"&gt;</code> returns a string from the DOM regardless of its type attribute, so you concatenated. Add <code>.number</code> — <code>v-model.number="age"</code> — and Vue parses it with <code>parseFloat</code> before writing to state.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Why can't you <code>v-model</code> a prop?"</strong><br>A: Because it desugars to an assignment to that prop, which breaks one-way data flow — the parent owns the value and would overwrite it on its next render. Vue warns. The fix keeps ownership with the parent: a writable computed whose getter reads the prop and whose setter emits <code>update:modelValue</code>, or <code>defineModel()</code>, which generates exactly that.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "How do multiple checkboxes bound to one <code>v-model</code> work?"</strong><br>A: If the bound state is an array, each checkbox pushes or removes its own <code>value</code> from it, so the array ends up holding the checked values. If the state is a boolean, a single checkbox toggles <code>true</code>/<code>false</code>, and <code>true-value</code>/<code>false-value</code> let you substitute other values. Radios are different again: they bind a single value, and it's the shared state — not a <code>name</code> attribute — that makes them exclusive.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<h2>Cheat-card</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><code>v-model</code> = binding + listener. text: <code>:value</code>/<code>@input</code> · checkbox/radio: <code>:checked</code>/<code>@change</code> · select: <code>:value</code>/<code>@change</code> · component: <code>modelValue</code>/<code>update:modelValue</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>Modifiers: <code>.lazy</code> (change not input) · <code>.number</code> (parseFloat) · <code>.trim</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><code>&lt;input type="number"&gt;</code> gives a <strong>string</strong> — always <code>.number</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>Checkbox → boolean, or → array when bound to an array. <code>true-value</code>/<code>false-value</code> for custom values.</li>`,
        `<li>Radios group by shared state, not by <code>name</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>Object options need <code>:value="obj"</code>, not <code>value</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>3.4+: <code>const model = defineModel()</code>. Named: <code>defineModel('start')</code> ↔ <code>v-model:start</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>Never <code>v-model</code> a prop — writable computed + emit, or <code>defineModel</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><code>@submit.prevent</code> on the form.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
      ].join('\n'),
    },

    {
      title: 'Class and Style Bindings, Event Modifiers',
      slug: 'class-style-events',
      order: 2,
      content: [
        `<h2>Why it matters</h2>`,
        `<p>These are the everyday mechanics of Vue templates — the things you type hundreds of times a week. They rarely form a whole interview question on their own, but they show up constantly inside larger ones ("write me a component that…"), and clumsy class handling is one of the fastest ways to make a code sample look junior.</p>`,
        `<p>Event modifiers in particular carry real substance: <code>.prevent</code>, <code>.stop</code>, <code>.self</code> and <code>.passive</code> map onto DOM concepts — default actions, propagation, event targets, scroll performance — that interviewers are genuinely testing when they ask "how would you close this modal when the backdrop is clicked?"</p>`,
        `<h2>The concept, simply</h2>`,
        `<p><strong><code>:class</code></strong> and <strong><code>:style</code></strong> are special-cased bindings. Unlike other attributes, they accept objects and arrays, and Vue <em>merges</em> them with any static <code>class</code> or <code>style</code> on the same element rather than replacing it.</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;!-- object: key = class name, value = condition --&gt;\n&lt;div :class="{ active: isActive, 'text-danger': hasError }"&gt;\n\n&lt;!-- array: a list of names or expressions --&gt;\n&lt;div :class="[baseClass, isActive ? 'active' : '']"&gt;\n\n&lt;!-- merged with the static class, not replacing it --&gt;\n&lt;div class="card" :class="{ 'card--wide': wide }"&gt;   &lt;!-- → \"card card--wide\" --&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<p><strong>Event modifiers</strong> are suffixes on <code>@event</code> that handle common boilerplate declaratively:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;form @submit.prevent="save"&gt;        &lt;!-- no page reload --&gt;\n&lt;div @click.self="close"&gt;            &lt;!-- only if the click hit THIS element --&gt;\n&lt;input @keyup.enter="submit"&gt;        &lt;!-- key modifier --&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<h2>How it works</h2>`,
        `<p><strong>Class binding</strong> resolves object keys whose values are truthy, flattens arrays (including nested objects inside arrays), and concatenates the result with the static class. On a <em>component</em>, the classes fall through to its root element and merge with whatever classes that element already has — which is what makes <code>&lt;MyButton class="mt-4" /&gt;</code> work without <code>MyButton</code> doing anything.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Style binding</strong> takes camelCase or kebab-case property names, auto-prefixes vendor properties where needed, and accepts an array of style objects to merge. Passing an array of values for one property — <code>{ display: ['-webkit-box', 'flex'] }</code> — renders the last supported one, a neat progressive-enhancement trick.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Event modifiers</strong> are compiled into wrapper code around your handler. The important ones, and what they actually do:</p>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><code>.prevent</code> → <code>event.preventDefault()</code> — stop the browser's default action (form submission, link navigation).</li>`,
        `<li><code>.stop</code> → <code>event.stopPropagation()</code> — stop bubbling to ancestors.</li>`,
        `<li><code>.self</code> — only fire if <code>event.target</code> <em>is</em> this element, not a descendant. This is the correct backdrop-click handler; <code>.stop</code> on the inner content is the clumsier alternative.</li>`,
        `<li><code>.capture</code> — listen during the capture phase, before the event reaches the target.</li>`,
        `<li><code>.once</code> — remove the listener after one call.</li>`,
        `<li><code>.passive</code> — tell the browser you will not call <code>preventDefault()</code>, so it can scroll without waiting for your handler. Meaningful for <code>touchmove</code> and <code>wheel</code> performance.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<p>Modifier <strong>order matters</strong>, because they wrap in sequence: <code>@click.prevent.self</code> prevents the default for every click and then filters; <code>@click.self.prevent</code> filters first and only prevents on self-clicks.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Key and mouse modifiers</strong> filter by which key or button fired the event: <code>.enter</code>, <code>.esc</code>, <code>.tab</code>, <code>.delete</code>, <code>.space</code>, <code>.up</code>/<code>.down</code>/<code>.left</code>/<code>.right</code>, any kebab-cased <code>KeyboardEvent.key</code> value, plus system modifiers <code>.ctrl</code>, <code>.alt</code>, <code>.shift</code>, <code>.meta</code> and the exclusive <code>.exact</code>.</p>`,
        `<p><strong><code>.passive</code> and <code>.prevent</code> are contradictory</strong> — passive promises you will not prevent the default, so combining them makes the browser ignore <code>preventDefault</code> and log a console warning.</p>`,
        `<h2>Code examples</h2>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;script setup&gt;\nimport { ref, computed } from 'vue'\n\nconst isActive = ref(true)\nconst hasError = ref(false)\nconst size = ref('lg')\nconst progress = ref(60)\n\n// Complex class logic belongs in a computed, not the template\nconst buttonClasses = computed(() =&gt; ({\n  'btn': true,\n  [\`btn--\${size.value}\`]: true,\n  'btn--active': isActive.value,\n  'btn--error': hasError.value,\n  'btn--disabled': !isActive.value || hasError.value,\n}))\n&lt;/script&gt;\n\n&lt;template&gt;\n  &lt;!-- object syntax --&gt;\n  &lt;div :class="{ active: isActive, 'text-danger': hasError }"&gt;…&lt;/div&gt;\n\n  &lt;!-- array, with a nested object --&gt;\n  &lt;div :class="['card', \`card--\${size}\`, { 'card--active': isActive }]"&gt;…&lt;/div&gt;\n\n  &lt;!-- static + dynamic merge into \"btn btn--lg btn--active\" --&gt;\n  &lt;button class="btn" :class="buttonClasses"&gt;Save&lt;/button&gt;\n\n  &lt;!-- style: camelCase or 'kebab-case' --&gt;\n  &lt;div :style="{ color: textColor, fontSize: size + 'px', 'background-color': bg }"&gt;…&lt;/div&gt;\n\n  &lt;!-- style array merges objects --&gt;\n  &lt;div :style="[baseStyles, overrideStyles]"&gt;…&lt;/div&gt;\n\n  &lt;!-- progress bar: bind a CSS custom property --&gt;\n  &lt;div class="bar" :style="{ '--progress': \`\${progress}%\` }"&gt;…&lt;/div&gt;\n&lt;/template&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;template&gt;\n  &lt;!-- ── Event modifiers ── --&gt;\n  &lt;form @submit.prevent="save"&gt;…&lt;/form&gt;\n\n  &lt;!-- ✅ The right way to close a modal on backdrop click:\n       fires only when the backdrop ITSELF is the target --&gt;\n  &lt;div class="backdrop" @click.self="close"&gt;\n    &lt;div class="modal"&gt;Clicks in here don't close it&lt;/div&gt;\n  &lt;/div&gt;\n\n  &lt;!-- Stop bubbling to a parent handler --&gt;\n  &lt;button @click.stop="remove"&gt;Delete&lt;/button&gt;\n\n  &lt;!-- Fire once, then remove the listener --&gt;\n  &lt;button @click.once="claimBonus"&gt;Claim&lt;/button&gt;\n\n  &lt;!-- Don't block scrolling while the handler runs --&gt;\n  &lt;div @touchmove.passive="onTouchMove"&gt;…&lt;/div&gt;\n\n  &lt;!-- Chained: order matters --&gt;\n  &lt;a href="/x" @click.stop.prevent="handle"&gt;Both&lt;/a&gt;\n\n  &lt;!-- ── Key modifiers ── --&gt;\n  &lt;input @keyup.enter="submit" @keyup.esc="cancel"&gt;\n  &lt;input @keyup.page-down="nextPage"&gt;         &lt;!-- kebab-cased KeyboardEvent.key --&gt;\n  &lt;input @keyup.ctrl.enter="submitAll"&gt;       &lt;!-- Ctrl held --&gt;\n  &lt;input @click.ctrl.exact="onlyCtrl"&gt;        &lt;!-- Ctrl and NOTHING else --&gt;\n  &lt;input @click.exact="noModifiers"&gt;\n\n  &lt;!-- ── Mouse buttons ── --&gt;\n  &lt;div @click.middle="openInNewTab" @click.right.prevent="showMenu"&gt;…&lt;/div&gt;\n&lt;/template&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<p>Common patterns worth stealing:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;!-- Passing the event alongside your own arguments --&gt;\n&lt;button @click="remove(item.id, $event)"&gt;Delete&lt;/button&gt;\n\n&lt;!-- Multiple handlers on one event --&gt;\n&lt;button @click="one(), two()"&gt;Both&lt;/button&gt;\n\n&lt;!-- Dynamic event name --&gt;\n&lt;button @[eventName]="handle"&gt;Dynamic&lt;/button&gt;\n\n&lt;!-- Conditional style with a fallback --&gt;\n&lt;div :style="{ transform: dragging ? \`translateX(\${x}px)\` : undefined }"&gt;\n\n&lt;!-- Classes fall through to a component's root element and merge --&gt;\n&lt;MyButton class="mt-4 w-full" /&gt;   &lt;!-- MyButton needs no code for this --&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<h2>Common mistakes &amp; gotchas</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><strong>Building class strings by hand.</strong> Template literals with nested ternaries are unreadable; use the object or array syntax, or a computed.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Assuming <code>:class</code> replaces the static <code>class</code>.</strong> It merges — which is usually what you want but surprises people expecting an override.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Using <code>.stop</code> where <code>.self</code> is meant.</strong> For a backdrop, <code>.self</code> on the backdrop is correct and simpler than <code>.stop</code> on everything inside it.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Combining <code>.passive</code> and <code>.prevent</code>.</strong> Contradictory: the browser ignores the prevent and warns.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Getting modifier order wrong.</strong> They wrap in sequence, so <code>.prevent.self</code> and <code>.self.prevent</code> behave differently.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Forgetting units in style bindings.</strong> <code>{ width: 100 }</code> is invalid; <code>{ width: '100px' }</code> is not.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Using <code>@keyup.enter</code> where <code>@keydown.enter</code> is wanted.</strong> For repeat-while-held behaviour, <code>keydown</code> is the right event.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Overusing inline <code>:style</code>.</strong> Prefer classes; reserve style bindings for genuinely dynamic values like positions, dimensions and CSS custom properties.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Relying on <code>.right</code> without <code>.prevent</code>.</strong> The native context menu still appears.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>Best practices</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li>Use the object syntax for conditional classes and the array syntax for lists; move anything with more than two conditions into a computed.</li>`,
        `<li>Prefer toggling classes over binding styles — it keeps presentation in CSS and cascades properly.</li>`,
        `<li>Bind CSS custom properties for dynamic values (<code>--progress</code>, <code>--x</code>) so the CSS file keeps the actual rules.</li>`,
        `<li>Use <code>.self</code> for backdrops and click-outside behaviour, and <code>.stop</code> only when you genuinely need to halt propagation.</li>`,
        `<li>Add <code>.passive</code> to <code>touchmove</code> and <code>wheel</code> handlers that never prevent the default — it measurably helps scroll performance on mobile.</li>`,
        `<li>Use <code>.exact</code> when a plain click and a modified click should do different things.</li>`,
        `<li>Keep handlers in <code>&lt;script setup&gt;</code>; inline expressions are fine for one statement, not for logic.</li>`,
        `<li>With Tailwind, list conditional classes in an array and let a helper handle merging conflicts rather than concatenating strings.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>⭐ Interview lens</h2>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "How do you apply a class conditionally?"</strong><br>A: Object syntax — <code>:class="{ active: isActive }"</code> — where each key is applied when its value is truthy. Arrays combine multiple sources and can contain objects. Both merge with the static <code>class</code> attribute rather than replacing it. Once the logic has more than a couple of conditions, I move the whole object into a computed so the template stays a single reference.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "How would you close a modal when the backdrop is clicked, but not when the modal content is?"</strong><br>A: <code>@click.self="close"</code> on the backdrop. <code>.self</code> fires the handler only when <code>event.target</code> is the backdrop element itself, so clicks that originated inside the modal and bubbled up are ignored. The alternative — <code>.stop</code> on the modal content — works but requires remembering it on every interactive child, so <code>.self</code> is the cleaner expression.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "What does <code>.passive</code> do and when do you use it?"</strong><br>A: It adds the listener with <code>{ passive: true }</code>, promising the browser you will not call <code>preventDefault()</code>. Without that promise the browser must wait for your handler to finish before it knows whether it may scroll, which causes jank on <code>touchmove</code> and <code>wheel</code>. Combining it with <code>.prevent</code> is contradictory — the prevent is ignored and you get a console warning.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Does modifier order matter?" (trap)</strong><br>A: Yes, because they compile into wrappers applied in sequence. <code>@click.prevent.self</code> calls <code>preventDefault()</code> on every click and then filters to self-clicks; <code>@click.self.prevent</code> filters first, so the default is only prevented when the element itself was clicked. Same two words, different behaviour.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "How do classes on a component work?"</strong><br>A: <code>class</code> isn't a declared prop, so it's a fallthrough attribute — Vue applies it to the component's single root element and <em>merges</em> it with whatever classes that element already has. So <code>&lt;MyButton class="mt-4" /&gt;</code> works with no code in <code>MyButton</code>. With multiple root elements Vue can't choose a target, so you need explicit <code>v-bind="$attrs"</code> or <code>:class="$attrs.class"</code>.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<h2>Cheat-card</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><code>:class="{ name: cond }"</code> · <code>:class="[a, b, { c: cond }]"</code> — both <strong>merge</strong> with static <code>class</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><code>:style="{ fontSize: n + 'px' }"</code> — camelCase or 'kebab-case'; arrays merge; remember units.</li>`,
        `<li>Bind CSS custom properties for dynamic values: <code>:style="{ '--x': x + 'px' }"</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>Event modifiers: <code>.prevent</code> · <code>.stop</code> · <code>.self</code> · <code>.capture</code> · <code>.once</code> · <code>.passive</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Backdrop click → <code>.self</code></strong>, not <code>.stop</code> everywhere else.</li>`,
        `<li><code>.passive</code> + <code>.prevent</code> = contradiction (warning).</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Order matters</strong> — modifiers wrap in sequence.</li>`,
        `<li>Keys: <code>.enter</code> <code>.esc</code> <code>.tab</code> <code>.space</code> <code>.delete</code>, any kebab-cased <code>event.key</code>; systems <code>.ctrl</code> <code>.alt</code> <code>.shift</code> <code>.meta</code>; <code>.exact</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>Mouse: <code>.left</code> <code>.right</code> <code>.middle</code> (pair <code>.right</code> with <code>.prevent</code>).</li>`,
        `<li>Component <code>class</code> falls through to the root and merges.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
      ].join('\n'),
    },

    {
      title: 'Custom Directives',
      slug: 'custom-directives',
      order: 3,
      content: [
        `<h2>Why it matters</h2>`,
        `<p>Custom directives are the answer to "how do I reuse low-level DOM behaviour?" — click-outside, autofocus, lazy-loaded images, tooltips, permission-based visibility, infinite scroll. They are also a test of judgement, because the honest answer to most "should this be a directive?" questions is <em>no, it should be a component or a composable</em>. Interviewers ask about them partly to see whether you know that.</p>`,
        `<p>Knowing when a directive is genuinely the right tool — plain DOM manipulation applied declaratively to arbitrary elements — is worth more than memorising the hook names.</p>`,
        `<h2>The concept, simply</h2>`,
        `<p>A <strong>custom directive</strong> is an object of lifecycle hooks that receive the raw DOM element. You register it, then apply it in a template with a <code>v-</code> prefix.</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;script setup&gt;\n// In &lt;script setup&gt;, any camelCase variable named vSomething is a directive\nconst vFocus = {\n  mounted: (el) =&gt; el.focus(),\n}\n&lt;/script&gt;\n\n&lt;template&gt;\n  &lt;input v-focus&gt;\n&lt;/template&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<p>The hooks mirror the component lifecycle but operate on <em>an element</em>:</p>`,
        `<table>`,
        `<thead><tr><th>Hook</th><th>Fires</th><th>Typical use</th></tr></thead>`,
        `<tbody>`,
        `<tr><td><code>created</code></td><td>Before attributes/listeners are applied</td><td>Rare — set up before native handlers</td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td><code>beforeMount</code></td><td>Before insertion into the DOM</td><td>Rare</td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td><code>mounted</code></td><td>Element is in the DOM</td><td><strong>The common one</strong> — measure, focus, attach listeners, init libraries</td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td><code>beforeUpdate</code></td><td>Before the containing component updates</td><td>Capture pre-update state</td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td><code>updated</code></td><td>After the component and children update</td><td>React to a changed binding value</td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td><code>beforeUnmount</code></td><td>Before removal</td><td><strong>Cleanup</strong> — remove listeners, destroy instances</td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td><code>unmounted</code></td><td>After removal</td><td>Final cleanup</td></tr>`,
        `</tbody>`,
        `</table>`,
        `<p>Each hook receives <code>(el, binding, vnode, prevVnode)</code>. The <code>binding</code> object is where the useful data lives: <code>value</code> (the expression's current value), <code>oldValue</code>, <code>arg</code> (the part after the colon in <code>v-tip:top</code>), and <code>modifiers</code> (an object from <code>v-tip.lazy.fast</code>).</p>`,
        `{{fig:ch5-custom-directive.svg|Anatomy of a custom directive usage v-tooltip:top.fast="message" broken into name, argument, modifiers and value, mapped onto the binding object passed to each hook|Every part of the directive syntax maps onto a field of the binding object your hooks receive.}}`,
        `<h2>How it works</h2>`,
        `<p>Directives are applied per element, and a single directive can be used on many elements in the same component. Vue calls your hooks at the corresponding points in the containing component's lifecycle, handing you the element to manipulate directly. There is no reactivity inside a directive — if you need to react to a value change, that is what the <code>updated</code> hook is for, and you compare <code>binding.value</code> with <code>binding.oldValue</code> yourself.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Registration</strong> is local or global. Locally, in <code>&lt;script setup&gt;</code>, a variable named <code>vFoo</code> is automatically available as <code>v-foo</code>. Globally, <code>app.directive('foo', definition)</code> makes it available everywhere — reasonable for a genuinely cross-cutting concern like <code>v-permission</code>.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>The function shorthand</strong> — passing a function instead of an object — registers it for both <code>mounted</code> and <code>updated</code>, which covers the common "apply this value to the element" case in one line.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Cleanup is your responsibility.</strong> A directive that adds a document-level listener in <code>mounted</code> and never removes it in <code>unmounted</code> leaks on every element it was applied to. This is the most common bug in hand-written directives and the thing to check in a code review.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>When <em>not</em> to write one</strong> — the judgement half of the topic:</p>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li>Rendering markup → that is a <strong>component</strong>. A directive that injects DOM is fighting the framework.</li>`,
        `<li>Reusable stateful logic → that is a <strong>composable</strong>. Better typed, testable as a plain function, and not tied to one element.</li>`,
        `<li>Conditional visibility by permission → arguably a component (<code>&lt;Can&gt;</code>) so the element is never rendered at all; a directive that hides it still ships it to the client.</li>`,
        `<li>Anything Vue already does → <code>v-show</code>, <code>v-if</code>, class bindings.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<p>The genuine sweet spot is <strong>plain DOM manipulation you want to apply declaratively to arbitrary elements</strong>: focus, click-outside, intersection observers, drag handles, third-party widget initialisation, text masking.</p>`,
        `<h2>Code examples</h2>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;script setup&gt;\n// ── v-focus: the simplest useful directive ──\nconst vFocus = { mounted: (el) =&gt; el.focus() }\n\n// ── v-click-outside: the classic, with correct cleanup ──\nconst vClickOutside = {\n  mounted(el, binding) {\n    // store the handler ON the element so unmounted can find it again\n    el._clickOutside = (event) =&gt; {\n      if (!el.contains(event.target) &amp;&amp; el !== event.target) {\n        binding.value(event)          // binding.value is the callback\n      }\n    }\n    // capture phase, so it still fires if a child calls stopPropagation\n    document.addEventListener('click', el._clickOutside, true)\n  },\n  unmounted(el) {\n    document.removeEventListener('click', el._clickOutside, true)\n    delete el._clickOutside           // ← forget this and you leak\n  },\n}\n&lt;/script&gt;\n\n&lt;template&gt;\n  &lt;input v-focus&gt;\n  &lt;div v-click-outside="closeDropdown" class="dropdown"&gt;…&lt;/div&gt;\n&lt;/template&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<p>Using arguments, modifiers and value together:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">// v-tooltip:top.fast=\"'Save changes'\"\nconst vTooltip = {\n  mounted(el, binding) {\n    const position = binding.arg ?? 'top'              // \"top\"\n    const fast = binding.modifiers.fast ?? false       // true\n    const text = binding.value                         // \"Save changes\"\n\n    el._tip = createTooltip(el, { text, position, delay: fast ? 0 : 300 })\n  },\n  updated(el, binding) {\n    // only do work if the value actually changed\n    if (binding.value !== binding.oldValue) el._tip.setText(binding.value)\n  },\n  beforeUnmount(el) {\n    el._tip?.destroy()\n    delete el._tip\n  },\n}</code></pre>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">// Function shorthand = mounted + updated. Good for \"apply this value\".\nconst vColor = (el, binding) =&gt; {\n  el.style.color = binding.value\n}\n// &lt;p v-color=\"theme.textColor\"&gt;…&lt;/p&gt;\n\n// Global registration for a cross-cutting concern\napp.directive('permission', {\n  mounted(el, binding) {\n    if (!auth.can(binding.value)) el.remove()   // ⚠️ see the caveat below\n  },\n})\n// &lt;button v-permission=\"'users:delete'\"&gt;Delete&lt;/button&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<p>A lazy-loading image directive — a case where a directive is genuinely the right tool:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">const vLazy = {\n  mounted(el, binding) {\n    const observer = new IntersectionObserver(([entry]) =&gt; {\n      if (!entry.isIntersecting) return\n      el.src = binding.value        // swap placeholder → real source\n      observer.unobserve(el)        // one-shot\n    }, { rootMargin: '50px' })\n\n    observer.observe(el)\n    el._lazyObserver = observer\n  },\n  unmounted(el) {\n    el._lazyObserver?.disconnect()  // ← the cleanup people forget\n    delete el._lazyObserver\n  },\n}\n\n// &lt;img v-lazy=\"product.imageUrl\" src=\"/placeholder.svg\" alt=\"…\"&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<p>When a directive is the <em>wrong</em> answer:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">// ❌ A directive that renders markup — that's a component's job\nconst vBadge = {\n  mounted(el, binding) {\n    el.insertAdjacentHTML('beforeend', \`&lt;span class=\"badge\"&gt;\${binding.value}&lt;/span&gt;\`)\n    // Not reactive, not escaped (XSS), invisible to the vdom, breaks on re-render\n  },\n}\n// ✅ &lt;Badge :count=\"n\" /&gt;\n\n// ❌ Reusable logic with state — that's a composable\nconst vFetchData = { mounted(el, binding) { /* fetch, store where? */ } }\n// ✅ const { data } = useFetch(url)</code></pre>`,
        `<h2>Common mistakes &amp; gotchas</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><strong>No cleanup.</strong> Listeners, observers and library instances added in <code>mounted</code> must be released in <code>unmounted</code>/<code>beforeUnmount</code>, or every element using the directive leaks.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Storing handler references badly.</strong> You cannot remove a listener without the same function reference; stash it on the element (<code>el._handler</code>) and delete it on cleanup.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Expecting reactivity.</strong> Directives are not reactive. React to changes in <code>updated</code> and compare <code>binding.value</code> against <code>binding.oldValue</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Doing work in <code>updated</code> unconditionally.</strong> It runs on <em>every</em> update of the containing component, not only when your value changed. Guard it.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Injecting HTML.</strong> Bypasses the virtual DOM, is wiped on re-render, and is an XSS vector if the value came from a user.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Using a directive on a component.</strong> It applies to the component's root element, and with multiple roots Vue warns and does nothing.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Hiding privileged UI with a directive and calling it security.</strong> The markup and any bundled data still reach the browser; authorisation belongs on the server.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Forgetting the naming convention.</strong> In <code>&lt;script setup&gt;</code> the variable must be <code>vCamelCase</code> to be usable as <code>v-kebab-case</code>.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>Best practices</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li>Reach for a directive only for direct DOM manipulation applied declaratively across arbitrary elements. Markup → component; stateful logic → composable.</li>`,
        `<li>Always pair <code>mounted</code> with <code>unmounted</code>, and write both at the same time.</li>`,
        `<li>Guard <code>updated</code> with an <code>oldValue</code> comparison so it does no work on unrelated re-renders.</li>`,
        `<li>Register locally unless the directive is genuinely global; <code>v-permission</code> and <code>v-tooltip</code> are the kind that earn global registration.</li>`,
        `<li>Use the function shorthand for the simple "apply this value on mount and update" case.</li>`,
        `<li>Prefix stored state on the element with an underscore and delete it on cleanup.</li>`,
        `<li>Check VueUse first — <code>vOnClickOutside</code>, <code>vIntersectionObserver</code>, <code>vElementSize</code> and others already exist, tested.</li>`,
        `<li>Type them with <code>Directive&lt;HTMLElement, ValueType&gt;</code> in TypeScript so <code>binding.value</code> is checked.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>⭐ Interview lens</h2>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "What is a custom directive and when would you write one?"</strong><br>A: An object of lifecycle hooks that receive the raw DOM element, applied declaratively with a <code>v-</code> prefix. The right use is low-level DOM behaviour you want to attach to arbitrary elements: autofocus, click-outside, lazy-loading images via IntersectionObserver, drag handles, initialising a third-party widget. If it renders markup it should be a component, and if it's reusable stateful logic it should be a composable — those two cases cover most of what people reach for directives to do.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Walk me through writing <code>v-click-outside</code>."</strong><br>A: In <code>mounted</code>, create a handler that checks whether <code>event.target</code> is contained by the element and, if not, calls <code>binding.value</code> — the callback the user passed. Add it to <code>document</code> in the capture phase so a child calling <code>stopPropagation</code> can't swallow it, and store the handler reference on the element so it can be removed later. In <code>unmounted</code>, remove the listener with that same reference and delete the stored property. Skipping that last part is the classic leak.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "What's in the <code>binding</code> object?"</strong><br>A: <code>value</code> — the current value of the expression; <code>oldValue</code>, available in <code>updated</code> and <code>beforeUpdate</code>; <code>arg</code>, the part after the colon, as in <code>v-tooltip:top</code>; <code>modifiers</code>, an object of the dot-suffixed flags; plus <code>instance</code> and <code>dir</code>. So <code>v-tooltip:top.fast="msg"</code> gives you position, speed and text in one declaration.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Directive or component or composable?"</strong><br>A: Directive for direct DOM manipulation on an arbitrary element. Component when there is markup to render or state to own — it participates in the virtual DOM, so it stays correct across re-renders. Composable for reusable logic with reactive state, because it's a plain function with proper typing and no element coupling. The failure mode is a directive that injects HTML: not reactive, not escaped, and wiped the moment Vue patches that node.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Is <code>v-permission</code> that removes an element a security control?" (trap)</strong><br>A: No. The component and whatever data it was bound to have already been sent to the browser, and anyone can re-add the element from devtools or call the API directly. It's a UX affordance — don't show people buttons they can't use. Real authorisation is enforced server-side on every request; the client-side check is a convenience layered on top.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<h2>Cheat-card</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li>Directive = object of hooks receiving the raw element. Local: <code>const vFoo = {...}</code> → <code>v-foo</code>. Global: <code>app.directive('foo', …)</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>Hooks: <code>created</code> · <code>beforeMount</code> · <strong><code>mounted</code></strong> · <code>beforeUpdate</code> · <code>updated</code> · <code>beforeUnmount</code> · <code>unmounted</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>Signature <code>(el, binding, vnode, prevVnode)</code>; <code>binding</code> = <code>{ value, oldValue, arg, modifiers, instance }</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><code>v-tip:top.fast="msg"</code> → arg <code>'top'</code>, modifiers <code>{fast:true}</code>, value <code>msg</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>Function shorthand = <code>mounted</code> + <code>updated</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Always clean up</strong> in <code>unmounted</code>; stash handlers on <code>el._x</code> and delete them.</li>`,
        `<li>Not reactive — diff <code>value</code> vs <code>oldValue</code> in <code>updated</code>, and guard the work.</li>`,
        `<li>Markup → component. Stateful logic → composable. DOM manipulation → directive.</li>`,
        `<li>Directives on components apply to the root element; multiple roots ⇒ warning.</li>`,
        `<li>Client-side permission directives are UX, never security.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
      ].join('\n'),
    },
  ],
}
